    {
      "id": "s05",
      "type": "BACKSTAGE_PHONE",
      "transition": "zoom",
      "background": "zoneA",
      "narration": "Say you want to know what language some repository is written in. Down the browser route, that's honest work. Boot a browser. Load the page. Wait for the whole DOM to settle. Meanwhile — over here — one call, straight to the server, and it's already back. Six hundred milliseconds. The browser hasn't even finished reading the text off the page it just spent five seconds loading. Same answer, either way. About fifty times faster one way than the other.",
      "data": {
        "backstagePhone": {
          "question": "what language is this repo in?",
          "stageLabel": "the whole play",
          "hopLabel": "the backstage phone",
          "hop": "api.get(\"/repos/microsoft/pw\")",
          "hopTime": "0.61s",
          "stageTime": "6.8s",
          "verdict": "~50x faster",
          "caption": "same answer. no stage at all.",
          "color": "green",
          "atWord": 3.667,
          "steps": [
            {
              "label": "launch a browser",
              "detail": "1.9s",
              "atWord": 11.5
            },
            {
              "label": "load the page",
              "detail": "3.4s",
              "atWord": 14.167
            },
            {
              "label": "wait for the DOM",
              "detail": "5.1s",
              "atWord": 17.667
            },
            {
              "label": "read the text",
              "detail": "6.8s",
              "atWord": 43.917
            }
          ],
          "hopAtWord": 28.167,
          "verdictAtWord": 49.167
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 862,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-16-api-testing_long_s05.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s06",
      "type": "CODE_RUN",
      "transition": "push",
      "background": "zoneB",
      "narration": "Here's what that looks like as actual code. You ask Playwright for a request context instead of a browser — that's the object that speaks HTTP and nothing else. Give it a base URL so you're not retyping the host in every test. Then get, post, put, delete, whatever you need. And notice the assertion at the bottom is a plain Python assert. No expect, no waiting. There's no DOM to wait for.",
      "data": {
        "codeRun": {
          "filename": "test_api.py",
          "language": "py",
          "resultLabel": "what comes back",
          "color": "blue",
          "atWord": 2.667,
          "caption": "no browser opens for any of this",
          "lines": [
            {
              "text": "@pytest.fixture(scope=\"session\")",
              "detail": "one request context, reused",
              "sub": "session scope, like ep 15",
              "label": "once",
              "atWord": 12
            },
            {
              "text": "def api(playwright: Playwright):"
            },
            {
              "text": "    ctx = playwright.request.new_context(",
              "detail": "this speaks HTTP, not clicks",
              "sub": "no browser is launched at all",
              "label": "http",
              "atWord": 18.75
            },
            {
              "text": "        base_url=\"https://api.github.com\")",
              "detail": "set the host once",
              "sub": "every call is a short path now",
              "label": "base",
              "atWord": 28.833
            },
            {
              "text": "    yield ctx"
            },
            {
              "text": ""
            },
            {
              "text": "def test_get_repo(api):"
            },
            {
              "text": "    resp = api.get(\"/repos/pw\")",
              "detail": "the actual call",
              "sub": "status, ok, json() come back",
              "label": "get",
              "atWord": 37.333
            },
            {
              "text": "    assert resp.ok"
            },
            {
              "text": "    assert resp.json()[\"lang\"] == \"TS\"",
              "detail": "a plain assert is fine here",
              "sub": "nothing to wait for",
              "label": "assert",
              "atWord": 49.083
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "durationFrames": 760,
      "audio": "audio/pw-dojo-16-api-testing_long_s06.mp3",
      "timingSource": "tts"
    },
    {
      "id": "s07",
      "type": "API_REQUEST_RESPONSE",
      "transition": "zoom",
      "background": "zoneA",
      "narration": "And what comes back isn't a page. It's a response object — a status code, an ok flag that's true for anything in the two hundreds, and a json method that hands you a Python dictionary. That's the whole surface. Three things.",
      "data": {
